cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: motifmultiverse
abstract: >-
  Bias-aware harmonization and robust inference of attribution-derived
  regulatory motifs across models and methods. An auditable attribution lexicon
  compiler: it turns several local TF-MoDISco outputs into one shared lexicon
  that is safe to compare across models, and records the merge, naming and
  admission decisions with their evidence rather than leaving them implicit.
  This pre-alpha implements one specification per inference run, not the
  specification multiverse the name anticipates, and its shipped adjudication
  criteria deliberately defer duplicate and fragment collapse rather than
  applying a merge policy no frozen document defines.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Wang
    given-names: Qiankang
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-2425-7940"
version: 0.1.0.dev0
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/wangkant/motifmultiverse"
keywords:
  - regulatory-genomics
  - motifs
  - attribution
  - tf-modisco
  - multiverse-analysis

# No DOI yet, and none is invented here. A placeholder DOI is worse than an
# absent one: it resolves to nothing while looking like a citable record. When a
# preprint exists, add its DOI under `preferred-citation` (type: article) so
# citations go to the paper; a Zenodo `doi:` for the software itself can only be
# minted once this repository is public, so it comes after that, not before.
